Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:12:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 096/119] fix: the wave glare's amount spans stops, not a 4x
 window - S7 read the pre-exposure as making sources brighter

The first runtime test found the analytic streaks working and the FFT glare gone
(TEST-CHECKLIST C4). Investigated before touching a number
(scene-notes/decisions/s7-glare-gain.md section 1): the path is NOT broken. Both
models share GetParams(), ResolveThreshold(), the same HDRStreakThreshold pass on
the same half-res fp16 source, the same Active flag and the same sprite/composite
plumbing. The kernel is a CPU FFT of the PUPIL - polygon, mask, barrel, spherical
aberration - so neither its normalisation nor its E_far tail can be mis-scaled by
an exposure change, and fNorm = 1/pitch^2 is pure geometry. What differs is
amplitude, and only the wave path pays the second factor:

  threshold 4.0 absolute -> 4 STOPS over grey (2.88): both models, ~5x
  amount divided by 50:                              wave only, 50x

270x against 5x is exactly "one still shows, the other is gone".

S7's premise was the error. "With the pre-exposure in place the sources are real
again, so the 50 is not needed" reads the pre-exposure as making sources
brighter; at a daylight exposure it DIVIDES by 3.4. The 50 came off and nothing
gave it back, and the surviving stored/50 mapping spanned 0 to 4x physical - which
cannot reach any case where 1x is not enough.

And 1x genuinely is not enough, correctly. A 200 kcd/m^2 practical (20.0 engine
units) at EV100 14.8: L_sat = 34 231, pre-exposure 0.292, exposed source 5.84 =
5.0 stops over grey; against the 4-stop threshold the excess is 2.96, the rays
take half a percent of it, and 0.0148 spread over a couple of thousand pixels is
some fifteen stops under mid grey. Nobody sees a sunstar off a desk lamp at noon.
The same lamp at a night exposure, or the sun four decades brighter, needs nothing
added at all. So the physics is not what wants changing - the control is.

Streak Amount under the switch is now GAIN IN STOPS, ten slider units per stop:

  0 -> off    50 -> physical (0 stops)    100 -> +5    150 -> +10    200 -> +15

The physical point does not move, so the shipped default, every saved level and
every CRC keep their meaning; the dial reads in stops like every neighbouring
control on this camera; and the range covers the five decades the physics spans.
Zero stays a hard off (the stage skips the pass) rather than flooring at 2^-5.
Approaches B (widen the divisor), C (raise the default instead - that belongs to
a lens preset, not to the engine) and D (auto-normalise - removes the exposure
dependence the whole pipeline exists to create) are on the page and rejected.

The once-per-change [BLOOM] line now prints the RESOLVED gain and its stops rather
than a number whose meaning depends on which mapping is in force. README,
docs/SceneReferredContent.md 6.3 and the component tooltip carry the worked
example and say out loud that 1x physical on a daylight-exposed 200 kcd/m^2 source
is faint by nature - so the next reader does not take a correct picture for a
broken one, which is what happened here.

